now guaranteed wide awake here in the  studio is today's guest Patrick Cowden  Patrick spent years as a high-flying  manager and he's now a best-selling  author on the topic of work and  corporate culture Patrick thanks very  much for joining us thank you now  firstly it seems that young people want  to have fun in the workplace and the  older generation seems intent on  continuing to work is that really the  case well I think fun is probably not  exactly the right word I think they're  searching for sense sense of purpose and  meaning and the interesting thing is  that connects both generations because  the older generation is looking for the  same thing they don't want to just quit  and stop everything their life purpose  has been about they want to continue so  I think connecting those two potentials  of the young the vitality and the older  with the wisdom that's an interesting  challenge for the future could that  potentially be also a nice way of  putting a spin on things for example we  do see a lot of young people who are  stuck in this vicious circle of unending  internships and then perhaps the older  generation who simply don't stop working  because they can't make ends meet  perhaps well I think you know jumping  from one internship to the next is a way  of expressing that search you know a  search for meaningfulness and I think  all the generations have had that but  the younger generation is a little bit  bolder about sticking to it longer and I  think that the older generation as they  can recognize the potential is coming  from from Gen Y I think it would  activate the older generation to to go  further and and to give more than they  have before and I think it's the  challenge to the organization's to find  in an environment that can activate that  for all the generations in the company  absolutely German companies have been  sounding the alarm about the the  potential of skilled workers retiring  and they're not having the young  professionals coming in to fill those  jobs so how do we retract it attract and  retain young talent well I think that's  one of the elements is attracting the  talent but I think the other element is  activating everything that's there  you know if 85 percent of people aren't  activated in quality and engaged the  bigger question for current organ  is not filling the smaller gap but  activating the bigger piece of the  organization across all generations men  and women everybody going further than  they've ever gone before that's fun  you've held leadership positions at  Deutsche Bank at Dell at Hitachi you're  somebody who knows what it's like to do  80-hour workweeks and have back-to-back  meetings for example so how did you  manage it personally well it's always a  challenge and I think over the years  that was eight years ago at Dell where  was the worst you know you realize that  everywhere in the world all the  different places I go you see that same  mechanism focus on profit focus on  results and it comes at a price  we lose connection to the things that  are most important to us and at some  point you get sick you get burnout and  that's not in the interests of the  company so the organizations need to  understand is there a way to change the  environment so that we activate the  goodness and not the badness and how  should they do that what needs to change  in corporate culture do you think well I  think understanding the drivers around  people in the quality of our teamwork  underneath all the cultural layers  elements like respect and trust and  appreciation they breed a strength and a  health that it's not about the hours we  do but the quality of how we do those  hours if we can come home with  motivation and engagement and feel  really good about the day that's a  quality that's not at the top of the  list of most companies today I think  that's the future of where we need to go  let's talk a little bit international  then we know that in Japan that there  can be quite a rigid working culture  than say for example here in Germany  that France can be hierarchical why is a  good place to work well I think the  workplace of the future crosses all  those different boundaries it's not just  oh Bob I am you know one part of one  country I think these cultures as they  come together in the globalization we  see a huge human potential underneath  that respect element that trust element  and I think if organizations can connect  that together that's where we want to  work we want to work where this quality  of teamwork is at a high level and our  engagement goes up every single day and  not the opposite effect that we see out  there today  that almost no one really wants to give  everything for their team or their  company anymore there's a brighter  future that we can strive for  I like that future better thank you very  much for joining us Patrick cowden Thank  You Helen
 

